ipq40xx: chromium: google_wifi (gale): declare the on-board ChromeOS EC

The gale EC is an I2C slave to the AP at 0x1e on blsp1_i2c4 (the 78b8000
bus, /dev/i2c-1 in Linux), speaking the cros_ec host-command protocol v3.
The stock manufacturer devicetree never declared it, so upstream OpenWrt
has no node and cros_ec_i2c cannot bind. Add the node so, with
CONFIG_CROS_EC{,_I2C,_CHARDEV} enabled (set from gale.config via the
CONFIG_KERNEL_ passthrough), the AP gets /dev/cros_ec and can issue host
commands (HELLO/GET_VERSION/USB_PD_CONTROL/USB_PD_PORTS — validated live
2026-07-18).

No AP-side EC interrupt is wired for host use, so the node omits
`interrupts`; cros_ec treats the irq as optional (host commands are
synchronous), so polled reads work without it.

Tested against: OpenWrt v25.12.4 (r32933-4ccb782af7).

--- a/target/linux/ipq40xx/files-6.12/arch/arm/boot/dts/qcom/qcom-ipq4019-wifi.dts
+++ b/target/linux/ipq40xx/files-6.12/arch/arm/boot/dts/qcom/qcom-ipq4019-wifi.dts
@@ -323,6 +323,18 @@
 	pinctrl-names = "default";
 	status = "okay";

+	/*
+	 * ChromeOS EC (gale_v1.1.5337). It is an I2C slave to the AP at 0x1e
+	 * on this bus, speaking the cros_ec host-command protocol (v3). Not in
+	 * the stock manufacturer DT; validated live 2026-07-18. No AP-side
+	 * interrupt is wired to us, so this node omits `interrupts` — cros_ec
+	 * treats the irq as optional and host commands are synchronous.
+	 */
+	cros_ec: ec@1e {
+		compatible = "google,cros-ec-i2c";
+		reg = <0x1e>;
+	};
+
 	led-controller@32 {
 		#address-cells = <1>;
 		#size-cells = <0>;
